WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.102
Multiply two expressions, each with two pieces.

2
00:00:03.102 --> 00:00:07.788
You could multiply every piece on the left by every piece on the right.

3
00:00:07.788 --> 00:00:13.464
That costs four multiplications. Can you get exactly the same answer using only three?

4
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:16.907
Use two plus three x, and four plus five x.

5
00:00:16.907 --> 00:00:20.696
The outside products are eight and fifteen x squared.

6
00:00:20.696 --> 00:00:23.770
The middle products are ten x and twelve x.

7
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:27.201
Together, those middle pieces give twenty two x.

8
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.913
Now add the two coefficients on each side first.

9
00:00:30.913 --> 00:00:37.333
Five times nine is forty five. This single multiplication contains all four earlier products.

10
00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:42.648
Subtract eight and fifteen, and the missing middle coefficient is twenty two.

11
00:00:43.033 --> 00:00:49.470
That is Karatsuba's trick. We paid for three products, and used additions and subtractions

12
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:55.764
to recover the answer. Our cost model counts multiplications between input coefficients.

13
00:00:55.764 --> 00:01:00.841
Additions and multiplication by fixed constants are free in this model.

14
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:05.680
Now change the arithmetic. Our coefficients are only zero and one.

15
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.319
After addition, keep the remainder on division by two.

16
00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:15.114
So one plus one becomes zero. Two identical contributions cancel, instead of adding up

17
00:01:15.114 --> 00:01:15.585
to two.

18
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:22.216
A number in our new system has four slots: constant, x, x squared, and x cubed.

19
00:01:22.216 --> 00:01:27.199
For example, one plus x cubed is the list one, zero, zero, one.

20
00:01:27.199 --> 00:01:31.471
These are polynomial coefficients, not decimal digits.

21
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:34.643
Multiplication can produce higher powers.

22
00:01:34.643 --> 00:01:40.060
We fold them back using a fixed rule: x to the fourth equals x squared plus one.

23
00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.883
For example, x cubed times x becomes one plus x squared, which fits in our four slots.

24
00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:51.213
The ordinary expansion has four times four coefficient products, so sixteen.

25
00:01:51.213 --> 00:01:54.598
Group each input into two smaller polynomial pieces.

26
00:01:54.598 --> 00:02:00.195
Apply the three-product trick outside, then again inside each of those three products.

27
00:02:00.195 --> 00:02:02.083
Three times three gives nine.

28
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:05.124
This proves that nine products suffice.

29
00:02:05.124 --> 00:02:07.918
But perhaps a different trick uses eight.

30
00:02:07.918 --> 00:02:10.644
Finding one recipe gives an upper bound.

31
00:02:10.644 --> 00:02:14.733
Ruling out every possible cheaper recipe is a different job.

32
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:17.323
We need a way to describe all recipes.

33
00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.438
A permitted product takes a sum from the first input and multiplies it by a sum from

34
00:02:23.438 --> 00:02:28.151
the second. For example, a zero plus a two, times b one plus b three.

35
00:02:28.151 --> 00:02:31.020
Its expansion marks four places in a grid.

36
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:36.709
Choose any nonempty selection of the four rows, and any nonempty selection of columns.

37
00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:42.018
There are fifteen choices each way, giving two hundred and twenty five possible grids.

38
00:02:42.018 --> 00:02:45.104
Mathematicians call each of these a rank-one form.

39
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.823
Why a grid? Each square represents one coefficient product, such as a two times b one.

40
00:02:51.823 --> 00:02:55.132
A coloured square means include that product.

41
00:02:55.132 --> 00:02:58.882
Combining grids means adding corresponding squares.

42
00:02:58.882 --> 00:03:02.852
In our binary arithmetic, two coloured squares cancel.

43
00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:08.449
The actual multiplication has four output coefficients, so four target grids.

44
00:03:08.449 --> 00:03:11.701
Here is the target for the constant coefficient.

45
00:03:11.701 --> 00:03:17.459
Its marked squares record exactly which input products contribute a constant after we

46
00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:18.881
fold the powers back.

47
00:03:19.267 --> 00:03:23.961
A multiplication recipe succeeds when every target grid can be made by adding some of

48
00:03:23.961 --> 00:03:27.330
its product grids. The same product can help several outputs.

49
00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:31.915
This is why counting the coloured squares alone cannot tell us the cheapest recipe.

50
00:03:32.297 --> 00:03:37.714
The set of all grids you can make by adding a chosen collection is called its span.

51
00:03:37.714 --> 00:03:42.479
Start with two simple grids: you can make zero, either one, or their sum.

52
00:03:42.479 --> 00:03:46.265
A new independent grid doubles these binary possibilities.

53
00:03:46.663 --> 00:03:50.707
Imagine a container holding all combinations of the chosen grids.

54
00:03:50.707 --> 00:03:55.621
This container is a schematic picture of a span, not an ordinary room in space.

55
00:03:55.621 --> 00:04:00.971
The four target grids already supply four independent directions that every successful

56
00:04:00.971 --> 00:04:02.215
container must hold.

57
00:04:02.597 --> 00:04:07.705
The paper proves that an eight-product recipe exists exactly when four extra product

58
00:04:07.705 --> 00:04:13.178
grids complete an eight-dimensional container that can itself be built from product grids.

59
00:04:13.178 --> 00:04:17.861
That equivalence is what makes the finite search cover every possible recipe.

60
00:04:18.230 --> 00:04:23.869
Without using symmetry, a checker examines one hundred and three million, four hundred

61
00:04:23.869 --> 00:04:28.197
and six thousand, six hundred and sixteen eligible four-grid sets.

62
00:04:28.197 --> 00:04:33.902
None works. A separate symmetry-based search reduces the work to seventy four thousand,

63
00:04:33.902 --> 00:04:36.590
three hundred and seventy seven prefixes.

64
00:04:36.963 --> 00:04:41.133
The important word is exhaustive. A failed hunt alone proves nothing.

65
00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:46.269
Here the written reduction proves that every eight-product recipe would appear in the

66
00:04:46.269 --> 00:04:51.525
search. Zero successful cases therefore rules out eight, while our construction already

67
00:04:51.525 --> 00:04:52.371
supplies nine.

68
00:04:52.763 --> 00:04:58.407
Change the coefficients to zero, one, and two, with arithmetic wrapping around at three.

69
00:04:58.407 --> 00:05:02.704
The answer changes to eight. For example, two plus two becomes one.

70
00:05:02.704 --> 00:05:08.219
A finite field is a finite arithmetic system where every nonzero number can be divided

71
00:05:08.219 --> 00:05:08.411
by.

72
00:05:08.797 --> 00:05:11.658
The paper gives the eight products explicitly.

73
00:05:11.658 --> 00:05:16.944
Each combines coefficients of the first input and the same combination of the second.

74
00:05:16.944 --> 00:05:22.230
This is called a symmetric recipe. Ordinary recipes may use different combinations on

75
00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.101
the two sides.

76
00:05:23.497 --> 00:05:29.638
To verify such a recipe, test the sixteen pairs made from one, x, x squared, and x cubed.

77
00:05:29.638 --> 00:05:33.157
Every larger input is built by adding these pieces.

78
00:05:33.157 --> 00:05:38.953
Because the recipe distributes over addition in each input, those sixteen identities

79
00:05:38.953 --> 00:05:39.505
suffice.

80
00:05:39.897 --> 00:05:44.989
For fields with three, four, or five elements, seven products are impossible.

81
00:05:44.989 --> 00:05:50.742
For five, two separate exact programs each examined seventy three thousand prefixes and

82
00:05:50.742 --> 00:05:53.388
over seventeen million residual buckets.

83
00:05:53.388 --> 00:05:58.017
Both found no seven-product recipe, and positive controls did succeed.

84
00:05:58.397 --> 00:06:04.130
Here is the complete answer: nine for two elements; eight for three, four, or five; seven

85
00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:08.188
for larger finite fields. The field size must be a prime power.

86
00:06:08.188 --> 00:06:12.053
A four-element field is not ordinary arithmetic modulo four.

87
00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:16.729
A second question uses six coefficients arranged in a circle.

88
00:06:16.729 --> 00:06:21.521
Multiply their polynomials, but now wrap x to the sixth back to one.

89
00:06:21.521 --> 00:06:27.652
Positions repeat every six steps. This operation is called cyclic convolution of length

90
00:06:27.652 --> 00:06:27.934
six.

91
00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:33.166
For a concrete example, multiply one plus x to the fifth by one plus x.

92
00:06:33.166 --> 00:06:37.797
Before wrapping, you get one, x, x to the fifth, and x to the sixth.

93
00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:40.930
The last becomes one, and the two ones cancel.

94
00:06:41.297 --> 00:06:46.816
The result is x plus x to the fifth. To calculate arbitrary inputs efficiently, the algebra

95
00:06:46.816 --> 00:06:51.364
splits this six-slot problem into a two-slot piece and our four-slot piece.

96
00:06:51.364 --> 00:06:54.761
They cost three products and nine products respectively.

97
00:06:55.130 --> 00:07:00.623
Adding those costs needs a theorem. Costs of independent pieces do not always add in

98
00:07:00.623 --> 00:07:06.116
this subject. The special theorem of Ja'Ja' and Takche applies because one piece has

99
00:07:06.116 --> 00:07:11.282
two input and output coordinates. Here it proves three plus nine equals twelve.

100
00:07:11.663 --> 00:07:15.088
What is new is narrower than the table might suggest.

101
00:07:15.088 --> 00:07:20.646
Rambaud already recorded the symmetric binary value nine in twenty fourteen and in his

102
00:07:20.646 --> 00:07:26.139
corrected thesis. The present binary exclusion also permits different combinations on

103
00:07:26.139 --> 00:07:27.431
the two input sides.

104
00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:34.339
The subspace search methods also build on Barbulescu, Detrey, Estibals, Zimmermann, Covanov,

105
00:07:34.339 --> 00:07:39.503
and Rambaud. The four-element value had already been claimed in a survey.

106
00:07:39.503 --> 00:07:45.870
The surviving contributions concern unrestricted binary rank, the explicit ternary recipe,

107
00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.134
and the convolution consequence.

108
00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:52.244
Multiple exact checkers and controls support the computations.

109
00:07:52.244 --> 00:07:56.019
Twelve compiled checks passed in the accepted research package.

110
00:07:56.019 --> 00:08:01.351
A separate logical certificate was verified too; its large proof file is archived outside

111
00:08:01.351 --> 00:08:04.346
this repository. No Lean formalization is claimed.

112
00:08:04.730 --> 00:08:10.423
Remember the chain: additions can replace products; grids describe every permitted recipe;

113
00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:14.282
exhaustive exclusion makes nine optimal in the binary system.

114
00:08:14.282 --> 00:08:19.596
Correctness confidence is high. Priority remains moderately confident and bounded by

115
00:08:19.596 --> 00:08:23.834
the sources checked. Larger multiplication systems still need work.
